WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.970
After a power cut last term, our

2
00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:05.050
office computer wouldn’t start,

3
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.350
two weeks before exams.

4
00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.190
The exam papers were on

5
00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.630
it, and only on it.

6
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.750
We’d worried about hackers all year,

7
00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.230
and a power cut nearly beat us.

8
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.100
Today: how to assess your school’s cyber

9
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.320
risks, and decide what to fix first.

10
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.480
Start with what you’re protecting.

11
00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.510
Pupil records and marks.

12
00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.230
Exam papers.

13
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:32.820
Fee records.

14
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:34.440
Staff email.

15
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.660
And the school’s social media

16
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.680
page, which parents trust.

17
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.000
Then ask what could go wrong.

18
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:43.490
Phishing messages.

19
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.630
A lost phone with no screen lock.

20
00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.100
Ransomware, or an old computer that no

21
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.520
longer gets security updates.

22
00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:53.810
Power cuts and floods.

23
00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.770
And someone inside who

24
00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.410
misuses what they can see.

25
00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.590
For each one, score two

26
00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.610
things from one to three.

27
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.330
How likely is it?

28
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.460
And how bad would it be?

29
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.160
Multiply them: that’s the risk

30
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.720
score, from one to nine.

31
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.020
It’s like a nurse in a busy clinic.

32
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.360
She doesn’t see the loudest

33
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.600
patient first, but the one most

34
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.440
likely to get seriously worse.

35
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.540
Fix the likely and serious risks

36
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.320
first, not the most dramatic.

37
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:28.220
Quick check!

38
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.380
We only have time to fix

39
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.660
one risk this week.

40
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.530
Which one comes first?

41
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.420
The unlocked office phone:

42
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.600
likely, and serious.

43
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.160
An office phone passes through many hands

44
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.000
and can go missing, and parents’ numbers

45
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.360
in the wrong hands mean scams.

46
00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.270
A jammed printer is likely but

47
00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.530
small, and the ministry’s

48
00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.990
servers aren’t yours to fix.

49
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.860
And a screen lock costs nothing.

50
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.320
For each top risk, choose a control.

51
00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.620
Prevent it: two-step

52
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.320
sign-in on staff email.

53
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.120
Detect it: someone checks the

54
00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.680
school page every day.

55
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.260
Recover from it: a weekly backup, and a

56
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.020
paper list of key phone numbers.

57
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.340
Write each one in a simple table:

58
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.340
the risk, its score, the control,

59
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.600
one owner and a date.

60
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.300
For example: the office computer has no

61
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.180
backup, score nine, a weekly backup on a

62
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.700
password-protected USB drive at the

63
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.240
bursar’s home, owner the bursar, done by

64
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:37.580
Friday.

65
00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.930
Then look at the table again every term.

66
00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:42.920
Your turn.

67
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.080
Someone checks the school

68
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.340
page every morning.

69
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.220
Does that prevent a

70
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:52.300
takeover, or detect one?

71
00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.610
Detect: it can’t stop a takeover,

72
00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.250
but it catches one fast.

73
00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.850
Remember: list what you protect, score

74
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.810
likelihood times impact, fix the likely

75
00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.430
and serious first, and give every

76
00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.290
control an owner and a date.

77
00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.470
Try it at school tomorrow: with

78
00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:15.130
two colleagues, list five risks on

79
00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.750
paper and score each one.

80
00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.170
Circle the top two, and

81
00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.250
give each an owner.
